{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we get to the episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a heads up we have two new items at the \nmerch store. There’s a link in the description"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7000,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cern's Large Hadron Collider routinely collides \nparticles at energies equivalent to a fraction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12360,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a second after the Big Bang. If this \nworries you, then the following fact will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either put you at ease or scare the hell out \nof you. And that's that a particle with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22440,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy of an LHC collision hits every square \nkilometer of the Earth every single second.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29160,
    "dDurationMs": 8060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we only relatively recently figured out \nwhere these cosmic rays are coming from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37220,
    "dDurationMs": 3340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "—-"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40560,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1912, Victor Hess took a hot-air balloon ride \nand got a Nobel prize. Hess had equipped the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balloon with devices sensitive to electrically \ncharged particles. As the balloon rose,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52760,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle detections increased, quadrupling by the \nmaximum altitude of 5 kilometers. Hess wondering  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if these might be charged particles coming \nfrom the Sun, and so repeated the experiment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at night and during a solar eclipse. The \nresults were the same. He concluded that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the vastness of space itself must be pelting \nus with energetic charged particles. In this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way cosmic rays were discovered. Hess’s \nNobel followed a couple of decades later."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we routinely measure cosmic rays with a \nwide range of advanced observatories—enormous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrays of ground detectors like the \nPierre Auger Observatory in Argentina,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90120,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or orbiting detectors on satellites and the \nISS. But for a long time the mystery only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intensified. We learned that the energies \nof cosmic rays span 12 orders of magnitude,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103360,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up to energies 10 million times that of the \nLarge Hadron Collider. The most energetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all—the so-called oh-my-god particle—had \nthe kinetic energy of a well-thrown baseball."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113800,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We did an episode on the oh-my-god particle \na while back, but didn’t get into the awesome  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118240,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "details of how cosmic rays are really produced. \nAnd we should probably cover that, because the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123240,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "origin of cosmic rays remained a mystery for \na century after they were discovered. It was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129040,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only a decade or so ago that scientists came to a \ngeneral agreement about the origin of most cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135400,
    "dDurationMs": 9480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays—the ones without the most ridiculous energies \nmostly come from exploding stars. From supernovae.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144880,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We still don’t know about the more energetic ones, \nbut it turns out that by understanding the complex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151840,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "process behind the supernova-generated rays we \ncan come up with possible mechanisms for the rest."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158480,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s see how exploding stars can act \nlike particle accelerators. For that we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need two things: shockwaves, and magnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shockwaves are a big part of what makes an \nexplosion an explosion, versus say a bunch of hot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173160,
    "dDurationMs": 8520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas expanding. They’re also a key ingredient in \nmaking cosmic rays. We’ll start with sound waves before we get to shockwaves. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181680,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are pressure waves—oscillating patterns of \nhigh and low pressure and density. We have some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initial event that compresses the air, creating \na high density region. That region pushes away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surrounding air molecules, creating a \nnew high density region down the path,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196200,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which pushes on the following region, and \nso on. The wave propagates at the speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199720,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sound while individual air molecules \njust move back and forth on the spot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204240,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A shockwave is what happens when the initial event \ncauses the particles to move faster than the speed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208760,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of sound in whatever medium they’re in. That \nmeans the particles will outpace any sound waves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and instead of bouncing backwards will continue \non, sweeping up the material they pass through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Familiar sources of shockwaves include supersonic \nvehicles or meteors, which push the air ahead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faster than the speed of sound. Or any explosion \nin which the expanding material is supersonic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Shockwaves in space are pretty common, both from \nexplosions and from objects moving faster than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238120,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "local speed of sound. Although it’s generally \ntrue that in space no one can hear you scream,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that doesn’t mean there isn’t sound in space. The \ngas between the stars may be incredibly diffuse,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it’s still there, which means it can support \nsound waves and so can also experience shockwaves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254360,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most famous type of space \nexplosion is the supernova. When the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "central core of a massive star has fused \nitself into a giant ball of iron and nickel,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262320,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it collapses into a neutron star and releases a \nprodigious amount of energy in neutrinos. These  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sweep up the outer layers of the collapsing core, \nsending them out a few percent the speed of light.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is much faster than the speed of sound in \nthe interstellar medium, so we have a shockwave."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a few percent lightspeed is not the \n99.999 etc % light speed of some of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284280,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic rays we’ve seen. It was Enrico \nFermi who figured out how supernova  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289840,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shockwaves can accelerate particles to \nvastly higher speeds than they themselves  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294760,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveled. This is the same Fermi who was \none of the founders of quantum mechanics,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built the first nuclear reactor, and \nwondered where all the aliens are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305000,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At around the same time that we were building \nour first particle accelerators on Earth using  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309120,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "powerful magnetic fields, Fermi realized \nthat magnetic fields might also do the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313600,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trick in supernovae. We all know that the \nSun has a magnetic field. When that field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gets tangled up, like it is right now, we \nget magnetic storms. These frequently erupt,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spewing out knots of magnetic field and \nenergetic plasma into the solar system.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the Sun can do that over and over \n.. imagine what an exploding star can do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think about magnetism for a minute. Moving \nelectric charges make magnetic fields with field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340040,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines that loop around the direction of \nmotion. And magnetic fields generate a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345120,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "force on moving charged particles that cause \nthose particles to circle around the field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349280,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines. The net result is that magnetic \nfields and the currents they generate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are self-reinforcing. In a plasma, magnetic \nfields sort of act like rebar in concrete,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358960,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reinforcing the plasma, giving \nit inertia, and also shaping it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363160,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So when a star explodes, \nits magnetic field doesn’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365680,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just vanish. Some gets locked in the \nresulting neutron star or black hole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369480,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the rest is dragged outwards with \nthe rest of the explosion. The expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shockwave is a tsunami of both charged \nparticles and of intense magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In an explosion in the atmosphere, the \nshockfront high-density of air smashes into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the lower-density air ahead of it, dragging it \ninto the propagating front. But the interstellar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387760,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "medium is so diffuse that we don’t expect a lot \nof collisions with an oncoming supernova front.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That would mean there is no shockwave\nif it weren’t for magnetic fields—but not just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400560,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those in the shockfront. Weak tendrils of \nmagnetic field suffuse the entire galaxy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weaving through the interstellar medium. Our \nsupernova shockfront first grabs this ISM magnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412920,
    "dDurationMs": 9200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and drags it forward, and this in turn drags the \nISM plasma. We call these collisionless shocks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422120,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think about the two components of the \nshockfront. We have the matter. Material from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the explosion is coming in fast. It slows down \nin the shockfront because this is where we’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "colliding with the interstellar medium. Meanwhile \nmaterial in the interstellar medium is being swept  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437120,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up and accelerated as it enters the front. So if \nyou’re a proton traveling with the shockfront,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your reference frame you actually see \nmaterial rushing in towards you from both sides. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Next we have the magnetic fields. Behind you \nthere’s the magnetic field from the explosion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which piles up as it slows down approaching \nthe front, and ahead you have the weaker  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458680,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interstellar field which is also piling up. \nThese result in two walls of magnetic turbulence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465600,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So our proton is chilling in the shockfront \nand bounces its way out, forwards or backwards,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it doesn’t matter. There it finds itself in \na magnetic tangle that’s effectively moving  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476360,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards it. It gets deflected back into \nthe front and is likely to pick up a bit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481360,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of energy in the process due to the relative \nmotion of the deflecting field. And then it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485880,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "goes out the other side of the front and gets \nturned around again, gaining a little more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489800,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy. This can happen many, many times, \naccelerating the proton to enormous speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495200,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Fermi Acceleration Mechanism, also \nknown as diffusive acceleration because the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles diffuse or bounce randomly through \nthe shock and tangled fields This is how we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506520,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turn a supernova into a particle accelerator. In \nfact the resemblance to an artificial particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512080,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerator is stronger than just the fact \nthat they both use magnetic fields. Why is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518440,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Large Hadron Collider so … what’s the word \n… Large? It’s because larger loops can keep hold  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of faster charged particles. The turn radius \nof a particle in a magnetic field is called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Larmor radius—or gyroradius or synchrotron \nradius—same thing. This radius increases with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537320,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speed of the particle and decreases \nwith the strength of the magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541440,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supernova remnants have magnetic fields not that \ndifferent to the Earth’s—really weak compared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to a typical particle accelerator. But \nthey can still accelerate particles to a large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fraction of the speed of light. The combination of \nweak fields and high speeds requires an extremely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "large Larmor radius. And so for supernova \nremnants to be good particles accelerators— "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564360,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without strong fields, they just need to be \nridiculously enormous. The turbulent regions can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extend many times the width of our solar system, \nwhile the overall radii of these expanding shells  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reach several light years. Plenty of space for \ncosmic rays to bounce their way to extreme eneregies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Supernovae can energize a proton up to 10^17  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588320,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electron volts. A supernova-accelerated \niron nucleus can reach about 10 times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that energy. But the maximum energy of a \nsupernova-accelerated particle is still  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599480,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 100 times lower than the highest energy \ncosmic rays. So where do the rest come from?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605920,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not so easy to tell where any \ngiven cosmic ray comes from because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609160,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we usually don’t see them directly. \nThey tend to collide with molecules  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612760,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the upper atmosphere and send showers \nof particles towards the ground. But by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collecting those particles we can get a \nrough sense of the origin of a cosmic ray."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Pierre Auger Observatory is a giant cosmic \nray detector that spans 3000 square kilometers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628280,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in western Argentina. It has found that \nthe origins of ultra high energy cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays are scattered across the sky. If they \nwere from sources within the Milky Way then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d expect a concentration in the plane of \nthe Milky Way’s disk. We can’t see exactly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643920,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where these particles are coming from, however \ntheir origin does appear to be correlated with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648760,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the large-scale structure of the universe. \nThey’re likely coming from other galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653840,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are many potential origins for the highest \nenergy cosmic rays—perhaps in the extreme magnetic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659480,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields around magnetars or around the supermassive \nblack holes in active galactic nuclei. These  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665360,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmic particle accelerators look a bit more like \ntraditional synchrotron accelerators like the LHC.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671840,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s also a strong possibility that these \ncosmic rays also come from scaled-up versions of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the supernova shockwave. Shocks are observed \neverywhere in the universe, and on all scales.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Enormous shockwaves result from collisions between \ngalaxies, from the most extreme stellar explosions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like hypernovae or gamma-ray bursts, and from \nthe jets produced by active galactic nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694600,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This last one is especially promising. When \na feeding and magnetized supermassive black  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699800,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole sends jets of material punching through its \nsurrounding galaxies, shockwaves are inevitable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Fermi mechanism will give us cosmic rays. \nAnd when those jets punch into the intergalactic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710880,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "medium beyond the galaxy, they can bloom into \ngiant lobes of diffuse plasma and magnetic field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717120,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although magnetic fields are weak in these lobes, \nthey span hundreds of thousands of light years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722440,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so are able to accelerate cosmic \nrays to the highest possible energies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726800,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It took a balloon ride and a century \nof sciencing to be fairly sure that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730840,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exploding stars are bombarding the earth with \ncosmic rays. It’s going to take a bit longer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735680,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fully understand the most energetic of \nthese particles. Whether they’ll turn out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be from magnetars or supermassive black \nholes or galaxy-scale magnetized shockwaves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the search is now on for the largest \nparticle accelerators in all of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752240,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do you do fellow simulations? Before we end \nthe episode, I just wanted to let you know that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757160,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are adding a couple new permanent items \nto the merch store. Back by popular demand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762000,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the return of the Be Quiet The Devs Will \nNotice T-shirt, which now also available for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768200,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the first time as a hoodie. And we also have \nour new How Do You Do fellow simulations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "T-shirt and sweatshirt. For those of you who'd \nlike to blend more seamlessly into the simulation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780240,
    "dDurationMs": 500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Whatever your stance is on your virtual existence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780740,
    "dDurationMs": 8635,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we've got you covered at our very virtual \nmarketplace over at pbsspacetime.com/shop."
    } ]
  } ]
}
